{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["In chess, openings are chosen partly for statistical win rates (inductive), whereas Popperian choice explicitly rejects statistical probability.","A courtroom verdict aims at finality/truth, while Popperian science aims at perpetual tentativeness."],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that 'surviving tests' logically increases the probability of future survival in a way that cannot be dismissed as mere convention.","If practical action inherently requires a belief in uniformity that cannot be bracketed as a mere decision."],"shared_structure":["Selection based on past resistance to attack rather than prediction of future invincibility.","Distinction between 'best available option' and 'guaranteed winner'.","Procedural rationality vs. outcome certainty."],"source_memory_refs":["courtroom_verdict_logic","chess_opening_selection"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a lawyer prefers a witness who has survived cross-examination without assuming they will never lie, a scientist prefers a theory that has survived tests without assuming it is true.","The rationality lies in the rigor of the testing process endured, not in the accumulation of positive instances."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories in practical action is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference of future truth, but as a non-inductive 'conventionalist decision' to adopt the theory that has withstood the most severe attempts at falsification, thereby maximizing the informational content and testability of our current knowledge base while acknowledging that this preference offers no guarantee of success.","counterconditions":["If the agent assigns a numerical probability greater than 0.5 to the corroborated theory based solely on its past survival.","If the preference is justified by an assumption that nature is uniform or that the future will resemble the past.","If the defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as a calculable metric rather than a regulative ideal.","If the distinction between 'acting on' a theory and 'believing' a theory collapses into an implicit inductive warrant."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Severity-Based Conventionalism: The mechanism distinguishes between the logical status of corroboration (a report of past performance) and the pragmatic rule of action (a decision to prefer the theory that exposes itself most boldly to refutation). By choosing the severely tested theory, the agent maximizes the potential for learning (if the theory fails) or retains the highest content statement (if it survives), without assigning it a higher probability of being true.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}